   "claims": [
    "The lowest career earned run average in history is 1.82, held by Ed Walsh, who played from 1904 to 1917.",
    "After the rules changed post-1920, the lowest career ERA score now belongs to Mariano Rivera (2.21), who played baseball from 1995–2013.",
    "Input these values to the ERA formula: ERA = (earned runs / innings pitched) × game innings ERA = (15 / 65.67) × 9 ERA = 2.06 The ERA score of this pitcher is equal to 2.06.",
    "The year 1920 brought a new baseball era with a significant change in rules (i.e., the introduction of the power hitter).",
    "MLB ERA leaders As mentioned above, there are two players with the best career ERA-score in history: Ed Walsh (1.82) and, after the change in rules, Mariano Rivera (2.21).",
    "An earned run average (ERA) of 0.5 signifies a highly effective pitcher.",
    "The all-time lowest career earned run average is 1.82, a record held by Ed Walsh, who played from 1904 to 1917.",
    "An ERA falling between 2.00 and 3.00 is considered excellent and is typically achieved by the top pitchers in the league.",
    "A range from 3 to 4 is above average, while a range from 4 to 5 is considered average.",
    "Assuming the game had 10 innings pitched and five earned runs, the earned run average (ERA) would be 4.50, which is considered an average result."
   ],
